{
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Light can act on an object because it carries energy.\nThe electromagnetic theory of Maxwell (麦克斯韦) describes light as an electromagnetic wave, so light energy is electromagnetic-wave energy.\nPhotometry studies light measurements related to visual response."
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "blockIndex": 1,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Different physical quantities describe the transfer of light energy, including luminous flux.\nPhotometry concerns the part perceptible to the human eye and evaluates optical radiation according to visual response.\nRadio waves have very long wavelengths, while $\\gamma$ rays have very short wavelengths; neither is directly visible to the human eye."
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "blockIndex": 2,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Luminous flux. Under ordinary photopic viewing conditions, the human eye is relatively sensitive to green light and does not respond equally to every wavelength.\nA measurement of radiant energy alone therefore does not describe its visual effect; that energy must also be evaluated according to visual response.\nThe resulting quantity is luminous flux, denoted by $\\Phi$."
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "blockIndex": 3,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Luminous flux is the optical-radiation energy passing through a surface per unit time, evaluated according to visual response."
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "In other words, luminous flux is optical-radiation power weighted according to visual response, not radiant power treated indiscriminately as visual effect."
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "A light source produces luminous flux, which reaches the surfaces of surrounding objects.\nThis process also requires luminous intensity, which characterizes the source, and illuminance, which characterizes the receiving surface.\nWe begin with the luminous intensity of an ideal point source."
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "blockIndex": 6,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Point source. A point-source approximation is appropriate when the source dimensions are much smaller than its distance from the position under study.\nThis lesson makes a separate idealizing assumption that the source emits uniformly in every direction; the point-source approximation alone does not guarantee that property.\nA distant star can illustrate the point-source approximation even though the star itself has a large volume.\nLike a particle, a perfectly rigid body, or an ideal gas, this model retains the important properties for the study and neglects secondary details."
    },
    {
      "index": 7,
      "blockIndex": 7,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Solid angle. A solid angle describes the directional region into which a source emits luminous flux."
    },
    {
      "index": 8,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Draw rays from a point to the boundary of an arbitrarily shaped region to obtain the conical region of space shown in Figure 134.\nIts solid angle is denoted by $\\Omega$; its boundary need not be circular.\nDraw a sphere of radius $R$ centered at the vertex. The solid angle $\\Omega$ cuts out a curved patch of area $S$ on that sphere.\nThe solid angle $\\Omega$ is the ratio of this spherical area to the square of the radius:\n$\\Omega=\\frac{S}{R^2}$ (6.1)"
    },
    {
      "index": 9,
      "blockIndex": 11,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "The solid angle subtended by the same set of rays is independent of the sphere radius, because the intercepted area $S$ is proportional to the radius squared.\nIts unit is the steradian.\nA solid angle of one steradian intercepts a spherical area whose numerical value equals the square of the radius expressed in the corresponding units."
    },
    {
      "index": 10,
      "blockIndex": 12,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "The solid angle subtended by all directions around a point is:\n$\\Omega=\\frac{S}{R^2}=\\frac{4\\pi R^2}{R^2}=4\\pi$ steradians (6.2)"
    },
    {
      "index": 11,
      "blockIndex": 13,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Luminous intensity. Luminous intensity describes the luminous flux emitted by a source per unit solid angle in a given direction.\nFor the uniform-emission model used here, within solid angle $\\Omega$ the luminous flux $\\Phi$ gives luminous intensity $I$ according to:\n$I=\\frac{\\Phi}{\\Omega}$ (6.3)\nIf intensity varies with direction, this ratio over a finite solid angle gives only the average intensity over that region."
    },
    {
      "index": 12,
      "blockIndex": 14,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "For an ideal point source emitting uniformly in every direction, the full solid angle is $4\\pi$ steradians, and therefore:\n$I=\\frac{\\Phi}{4\\pi}$ (6.4)"
    },
    {
      "index": 13,
      "blockIndex": 15,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Here $\\Phi$ is the total luminous flux emitted by the source in all directions, or total optical-radiation power evaluated according to visual response."
    },
    {
      "index": 14,
      "blockIndex": 16,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "The International System of Units uses the candela as the unit of luminous intensity①.\nThe original book describes a special light emitter as a measurement standard, for example for 1 candela; this description does not mean a measurement standard may be chosen arbitrarily.\nOther photometric units, including the luminous-flux unit, can be derived from the luminous-intensity unit."
    },
    {
      "index": 15,
      "blockIndex": 17,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "① The original footnote records the older Chinese name translated as candlepower; this lesson uses the name candela."
    },
    {
      "index": 16,
      "blockIndex": 18,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Unit of luminous flux. Luminous flux is measured in lumens.\nAn ideal point source with a luminous intensity of 1 candela, emitting uniformly over the directional region considered, emits 1 lumen within a solid angle of 1 steradian."
    }
  ],
